#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-06.png", width: 5pt), 262pt, 80pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 83.9pt, "the accumulated phase shift scales as ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(244.85pt, 83.9pt, "𝜑~")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(262.13pt, 77.42pt, "1")
  #line-at(262.13pt, 89.42pt, "𝜆")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(267.05pt, 83.9pt, ", so decreasing λ enhances overall phase sensitivity. For ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 99.74pt, "submicrometer features, the scattering efficiency increases toward the DUV and reaches a maximum ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 112.46pt, "when the wavelength approaches the feature size, increasing the in-line holographic signal. Importantly, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 125.06pt, "many biomolecules and materials exhibit strong and spectrally distinct absorption bands in the DUV. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 137.66pt, "This wavelength-dependent absorption provides a powerful source of intrinsic chemical contrast, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 150.38pt, "effectively acting as a label-free molecular “stain.” Together, these effects can reveal structures that are ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 162.98pt, "weak or invisible in the VIS range and can convert purely phase contrast into mixed phase–amplitude ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 175.7pt, "contrast. To quantify these effects and assess how PSR facilitates the recovery of fine phase details, we ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 188.42pt, "used a custom phase resolution target (Lyncée Tec, Boroflat 33 glass, height 125±5 nm). ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 214.94pt, "The top panel of Fig. 3 shows the full FOV phase reconstruction of the phase resolution target. The pink ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 227.66pt, "square indicates the region that was analyzed in greater detail, shown on the right. A single-frame ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 240.29pt, "reconstruction obtained using the AS method resolves the smallest designed features of the target, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 252.89pt, "whereas the corresponding PSR reconstruction additionally reveals submicron structures, such as small ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 265.61pt, "debries particles (marked with arrows), that remain invisible in the AS reconstruction alone. In this ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 278.21pt, "experiment, PSR was implemented in the multi-wavelength mode. For both VIS and DUV ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 290.93pt, "measurements, 22 holograms were recorded with a wavelength step of Δλ = 3 nm. The VIS sweep ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 303.65pt, "covered λ = 547–610 nm, while the DUV sweep spanned λ = 238–301 nm.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 330.17pt, "As expected for a purely phase object, the VIS measurements (shown below the FOV) exhibit strong ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 342.77pt, "contrast in the phase channel, while the amplitude shows only a contrast weak modulation. Contrary, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 355.49pt, "DUV illumination renders the same structures clearly visible in both phase and amplitude, reflecting the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 368.09pt, "increased amplitude and scattering, and higher phase sensitivity at shorter wavelengths. This confirms ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 380.81pt, "that transparent features effectively phase-only in the VIS acquire measurable amplitude contrast in the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 393.43pt, "DUV. The structures adjacent to the letter “S” correspond to a 2 μm linewidth, which is the smallest ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 406.15pt, "element on our phase target. The ultimate resolution of the system is analyzed in the following section. ")
]

