// arXiv source page 23
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 15.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 85.7pt, "Supplementary Document 1 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 119.18pt, "Low photon budget DUV lensless holographic (LH-DUV) reconstruction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 139.58pt, "In this supplementary experiment, we quantitatively evaluated the influence of single-hologram angular ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 152.3pt, "spectrum (AS) propagation, multi-frame pixel super-resolution (PSR) reconstruction, and PSR ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 164.9pt, "augmented with total-variation (TV) denoising on the noise level in LH-DUV reconstructions. Noise ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 177.62pt, "was quantified as the standard deviation (STD) of the intensity within a background region of a USAF ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 190.22pt, "resolution target (an area without any object features). For PSR and PSR with TV, the STD was ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 202.82pt, "computed from 1,674,240 pixels, whereas for AS the number of pixels was four times smaller due to the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 215.54pt, "upsampling factor inherent to the PSR algorithm. All results summarized in Table 1 correspond to the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 228.26pt, "same datasets used for Figure 2 of the main manuscript. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 248.81pt, "As shown in Table 1, the exposure time of 0.3 ms - representing the most challenging case, in which a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 261.53pt, "single hologram contains only three effective gray levels - yields the highest noise level when ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 274.13pt, "reconstructed with AS. Applying PSR to these data reduces noise by approximately 30%, which already ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 286.73pt, "constitutes a substantial improvement arising from multi-frame averaging. When TV denoising is ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 299.45pt, "additionally incorporated, the noise level decreases by a further factor of three relative to PSR alone. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 312.05pt, "For an exposure of 0.5 ms, a similar trend is observed: PSR reduces noise by about 30% (from 25.41 to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 324.77pt, "17.40), and the application of PSR with TV yields an additional ~40% reduction. The same relationship ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 337.37pt, "persists for the 1 ms dataset (third row), where PSR again provides an initial ~30% noise reduction, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 349.97pt, "followed by a further three-fold decrease when TV denoising is applied. Importantly, this dataset also ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 362.81pt, "achieves the final spatial resolution obtained under standard recording conditions (long exposure times). ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 383.33pt, "The final row of Table 1 presents results for long-exposure, high-quality data containing approximately ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 395.95pt, "150 gray levels. Under these conditions, PSR reduces noise by ~40%. As expected, the contribution of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 408.67pt, "TV denoising is smaller here (approximately 14%), which reflects the already low intrinsic noise level ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 421.27pt, "of the long-exposure high-photon-budget raw data. Nonetheless, it should be emphasized that the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 433.99pt, "proposed algorithm was specifically designed to suppress noise in extremely challenging low-photon-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 446.59pt, "budget conditions - scenarios in which phototoxicity is a concern and illumination reaching the detector ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 459.19pt, "must be minimized to avoid biological damage. Despite this specialization, the method remains effective ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 472.03pt, "when applied to standard, high-quality datasets. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 492.55pt, "Overall, these results demonstrate that while PSR alone consistently decreases noise across all ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 505.15pt, "illumination conditions, the incorporation of TV denoising provides a additional benefit for extremely ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 517.87pt, "noisy holograms, reducing noise by up to a factor of three while preserving spatial resolution, as also ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 530.47pt, "illustrated in Figure 2 of the Results section. Proper selection of TV parameters is essential to match the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 543.19pt, "noise level of the data; when chosen appropriately, PSR with TV achieves substantial noise suppression ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 555.82pt, "without compromising image detail. As the strength of TV denoising is adjusted to the data at hand ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 568.42pt, "calculated STD values are heterogenous with respect to the exposure time. In other words, column PSR ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 581.14pt, "with TV in Table 1 has no intrinsic significant trend due to manual adjustment of the TV parameters. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 593.74pt, "However, all values are significantly lower in comparison with both remaining columns which ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 606.58pt, "quantitatively assesses the proposed method as favourable. ")
]

